Part 2: Supported Instructions & Opcode Mapping
To build a processor that can run a real C/Assembly program (like your AES math routines), we will support a lean, high-impact subset of the RV32I base instruction set.
Every RISC-V instruction is exactly 32 bits wide. The last 7 bits ([6:0]) always represent the opcode, which tells the Control Unit the general class of the instruction.
Here are the specific instructions grouped by type that you should implement, along with their control field values:
1. R-Type (Register-to-Register Operations)
Used for arithmetic operations where both inputs are registers.
•	Opcodes to support: ADD, SUB, AND, OR, XOR, SLL (Shift Left Logical), SRL (Shift Right Logical).
•	Opcode bits [6:0]: 0110011
•	How the hardware differentiates them: They share the same opcode, so your Control Unit must look at funct3 (bits [14:12]) and funct7 (bits [31:25]).
Instruction	Opcode [6:0]	funct3	funct7
ADD	0110011	000	0000000
SUB	0110011	000	0100000
SLL	0110011	001	0000000
XOR	0110011	100	0000000
SRL	0110011	101	0000000
OR	0110011	110	0000000
AND	0110011	111	0000000
2. I-Type (Immediate & Load Operations)
Used for operations involving a register and a constant value encoded right inside the instruction, or for loading data from memory.
•	Opcode [6:0] for Arithmetic (e.g., ADDI): 0010013
•	Opcode [6:0] for Load (e.g., LW - Load Word): 0000011
Instruction	Opcode [6:0]	funct3	Description
ADDI	0010013	000	Add Immediate: reg[rd] = reg[rs1] + imm
ANDI	0010013	111	Bitwise AND with Immediate
ORI	0010013	110	Bitwise OR with Immediate
LW	0000011	010	Load Word from memory address (reg[rs1] + imm)
3. S-Type (Store Operations)
Used to write data from a register out into data memory.
•	Opcode to support: SW (Store Word)
•	Opcode bits [6:0]: 0100011
•	Hardware Challenge: S-Type split the 12-bit immediate value into two pieces across the instruction (bits [31:25] and bits [11:7]) to keep the input register source pins (rs1 and rs2) in the exact same spot as R-type instructions. This keeps the physical wiring tidy.
Instruction	Opcode [6:0]	funct3	Description
SW	0100011	010	Store Word: Memory[reg[rs1] + imm] = reg[rs2]
4. B-Type (Conditional Branching)
Used to alter program flow if a condition is met.
•	Opcodes to support: BEQ (Branch if Equal), BNE (Branch if Not Equal).
•	Opcode bits [6:0]: 1100011
•	Hardware Challenge: The immediate value represents a signed PC-relative offset. The bits are heavily scrambled in the instruction to maximize hardware efficiency. Your Immediate Generator will need to reconstruct it carefully.
Instruction	Opcode [6:0]	funct3	Condition
BEQ	1100011	000	Branch if reg[rs1] == reg[rs2]
BNE	1100011	001	Branch if reg[rs1] != reg[rs2]
5. U-Type (Upper Immediate Operations)
Used to load large 20-bit constants into the upper bits of a register. Essential for setting up memory base addresses for your Memory-Mapped I/O (MMIO) boundary later.
•	Opcode to support: LUI (Load Upper Immediate)
•	Opcode bits [6:0]: 0110111
Instruction	Opcode [6:0]	Description
LUI	0110111	Places 20-bit immediate in bits [31:12] of rd; zeros out [11:0].
6. J-Type (Unconditional Jumps)
Used for function calls and loops.
•	Opcode to support: JAL (Jump and Link)
•	Opcode bits [6:0]: 1101111
Instruction	Opcode [6:0]	Description
JAL	1101111	Stores PC + 4 in rd (return address), jumps to PC + imm
Your Next Step: Preparing for Day 2 (The ALU)
Now that you have mapped out exactly what opcodes, fields (funct3/funct7), and register behaviors your system must support, you can plan out Day 2's Arithmetic Logic Unit.
Based on the instruction list above, your ALU needs an input control signal (let's call it ALUControl) generated by your Control Unit. The ALU must look at that signal and execute one of these operations:
1.	Add (For ADD, ADDI, LW, SW)
2.	Subtract (For SUB, BEQ, BNE)
3.	Bitwise AND (For AND, ANDI)
4.	Bitwise OR (For OR, ORI)
5.	Bitwise XOR (For XOR)
6.	Shift Left Logical (For SLL)
7.	Shift Right Logical (For SRL)

